#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:1
msgid "Release 2.15 (2023-04-11)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:3
msgid ""
"Commands which take installables on the command line can now read them from "
"the standard input if passed the `--stdin` flag. This is primarily useful "
"when you have a large amount of paths which exceed the OS argument limit."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:7
msgid ""
"The `nix-hash` command now supports Base64 and SRI. Use the flags `--base64` "
"or `--sri` to specify the format of output hash as Base64 or SRI, and `--to-"
"base64` or `--to-sri` to convert a hash to Base64 or SRI format, "
"respectively."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:11
msgid ""
"As the choice of hash formats is no longer binary, the `--base16` flag is "
"also added to explicitly specify the Base16 format, which is still the "
"default."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:14
msgid ""
"The special handling of an [installable](../command-ref/new-cli/nix."
"md#installables) with `.drv` suffix being interpreted as all of the given "
"[store derivation](../glossary.md#gloss-store-derivation)'s output paths is "
"removed, and instead taken as the literal store path that it represents."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:16
msgid ""
"The new `^` syntax for store paths introduced in Nix 2.13 allows explicitly "
"referencing output paths of a derivation. Using this is better and more "
"clear than relying on the now-removed `.drv` special handling."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:19
msgid "For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:24
msgid "now gives info about the derivation itself, while"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:29
msgid "provides information about each of its outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:31
msgid "The experimental command `nix describe-stores` has been removed."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:33
msgid ""
"Nix stores and their settings are now documented in [`nix help-stores`](../"
"command-ref/new-cli/nix3-help-stores.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:35
msgid ""
"Documentation for operations of `nix-store` and `nix-env` are now available "
"on separate pages of the manual. They include all common options that can be "
"specified and common environment variables that affect these commands."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:38
msgid "These pages can be viewed offline with `man` using"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:40
msgid "`man nix-store-<operation>` and `man nix-env-<operation>`"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:41
msgid "`nix-store --help --<operation>` and `nix-env --help --<operation>`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:43
msgid ""
"Nix when used as a client now checks whether the store (the server) trusts "
"the client. (The store always had to check whether it trusts the client, but "
"now the client is informed of the store's decision.) This is useful for "
"scripting interactions with (non-legacy-ssh) remote Nix stores."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:47
msgid "`nix store ping` and `nix doctor` now display this information."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:49
msgid ""
"The new command `nix derivation add` allows adding derivations to the store "
"without involving the Nix language. It exists to round out our collection of "
"basic utility/plumbing commands, and allow for a low barrier-to-entry way of "
"experimenting with alternative front-ends to the Nix Store. It uses the same "
"JSON layout as `nix derivation show`, and is its inverse."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:53
msgid ""
"`nix show-derivation` has been renamed to `nix derivation show`. This "
"matches `nix derivation add`, and avoids bloating the top-level namespace. "
"The old name is still kept as an alias for compatibility, however."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.15.md:57
msgid ""
"The `nix derivation {add,show}` JSON format now includes the derivation name "
"as a top-level field. This is useful in general, but especially necessary "
"for the `add` direction, as otherwise we would need to pass in the name out "
"of band for certain cases."
msgstr ""
